                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 39,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Certainly your chassis doesn't have a fan connector.  Fan connector would go on the baseboard.\nhere: https://github.com/openbmc/entity-manager/blob/c88a1bea872d49a1ac7f4e0600f347120840486c/configurations/tyan/s8030_baseboard.json#L418"
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 39,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Moved them to the baseboard \ud83d\udc4d"
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 252,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "? Why are we naming something \"Ignore\"?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 252,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Because that sensor is not working. Only the second sensor is working."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 252,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Sounds like we need to go debug this patch a little more then?  Maybe omit this for now while it's not working?\n\nNaming something \"ignore\" isn't really a great mechanism to disable a channel.  That's probably worth talking through for a TMP421 given it does a \"name1\" approach."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 252,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "To elaborate more, because the TMP421 uses the hwmontemp sensor, which initially expected 1:1 mapping on sensor names, it's hard to disable the tmp421 itself.\n\nAre we positive we got the part number right here?  If we were using this driver, and the real part was something like a MAX1618, which doesn't have a local register, only remote, we'd get the behavior you're seeing here, where only one sensor would read."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 252,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> Naming something \"ignore\" isn't really a great mechanism to disable a channel. \n\nWhat is the mechanism to disable the channel, is there a way to do it?\n\nIf there is no way, let's not get blocked on that. If someone finds the 'right' way to do it then i can simply re-test that on my board.\n\n> Are we positive we got the part number right here?\n\nNot certain. That's simply the driver which happened to work on it. Someone at Nvidia will know it better since mellanox was apparently acquired by nvidia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mellanox_Technologies).\n\n> If we were using this driver, and the real part was something like a MAX1618, which doesn't have a local register, only remote, we'd get the behavior \nyou're seeing here, where only one sensor would read.\n\n```\nroot@s8030-bmc-30303035c0c1:~# zcat /proc/config.gz | grep -i MAX16\n# CONFIG_SENSORS_MAX16065 is not set\nCONFIG_SENSORS_MAX1619=y\n# CONFIG_SENSORS_MAX1668 is not set\n# CONFIG_SENSORS_MAX16064 is not set\n# CONFIG_SENSORS_MAX16601 is not set\n```\n\n```\nroot@s8030-bmc-30303035c0c1:/sys/bus/i2c/devices/i2c-19# echo max1619 0x1f > new_device\n\n[ 1083.670610] i2c i2c-19: new_device: Instantiated device max1619 at 0x1f\n\nroot@s8030-bmc-30303035c0c1:/sys/bus/i2c/devices/i2c-19# cat 19-001f/hwmon/hwmon5/temp1_input \n0\nroot@s8030-bmc-30303035c0c1:/sys/bus/i2c/devices/i2c-19# cat 19-001f/hwmon/hwmon5/temp2_input \n48000\n```\n\nso max1618 did not probe at all but max1619 driver did probe.\nI'm not sure if max1618 should be a separate driver, i only found max1619 in kernel \n\nhttps://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/9702969978695d9a699a1f34771580cdbb153b33/drivers/hwmon/max1619.c#L55\n\nAs you can see the max1619 does expose 2 temperature sensors so we're back at square one.\n\nI split the patch, one for the chassis and one for the NIC to avoid getting everything stuck here."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 252,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "I'll consider this one done since one of the sensors is working and i don't have further information or feedback to improve the config further. I'm happy for any new ideas but for now that's what's been working for me."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 252,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> What is the mechanism to disable the channel, is there a way to do it?\n\nToday there's not a way, but lets not call the sensor \"ignore\", which was the original request.\n\n> That's simply the driver which happened to work on it.\n\nLets not guess here.  If you have the board it's easy enough to find the chip and look up the part number.  If we need a new driver, so be it, but lets not check in a config that we know is incorrect.\n\nIf you don't want to solve that immediately, lets just omit the temp sensor for now, and we can regroup when we figure out the correct device."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 252,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "I'll mark this resolved since the NIC temperate sensors have moved to the other patch https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/entity-manager/+/87502 and you already opened a comment there."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 252,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "ACK."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 253,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Nic1 temp would belong to a NIC object?  Probably missing a file for the NIC?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 253,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Yep. ConnectX-6 Dx 100GE 2P NIC\n\nCreated a new file for that one \ud83d\udc4d"
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 295,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "You're sure this is what's in the FRU?  Do we need to wildcard any of this, or is it always going to be 1.57?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 295,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "I dumped the `ipmitool fru` output, so i am not understanding the question here."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 295,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Please don't resolve comments if you're asking a question back.  It makes it hard to know if you've resolved the issue or not, or are wanting more discussion.\n\nThis specific system you're on had \"1.57\".  The question is whether all of these systems have 1.57 in the FRU, or is 1.57 a version number, and we need to accept 1.X or 1.\\d\\d instead."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 295,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> The question is whether all of these systems have 1.57 in the FRU, or is\n 1.57 a version number, and we need to accept 1.X or 1.\\d\\d instead.\n\nI have another chassis `mbx-1.60-chassis.json` which is based on Tyan S5549 baseboard.\n\nSince that's an intel platform and S8030 is AMD, it cannot be a version number since it's different socket which means whole different board instead of minor changes.\n\nThat's my current understanding of things. If someone else has these boards and knows better i can change it but i do not want to introduce some wildcard match here based purely on speculations."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mbx/mbx-1.57-chassis.json",
                    "line": 295,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "ACK."
                }
            ],
