                {
                    "file": "configurations/mellanox/connectx-6.json",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "If I believe the manual here:\nhttps://docs.nvidia.com/nvidia-connectx-6-ethernet-adapter-cards-user-manual.pdf\n\nThis looks to be a 128kb part (A 16 bit part), so presumably we'd need to use an AT24c128 specific type here?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/mellanox/connectx-6.json",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for digging this up. Changed the type to match and added the document link in the commit message."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/tyan/s8030_baseboard.json",
                    "line": 419,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I count 5 fan headers on an S8030 board if I look at this picture https://www.mitaccomputing.com/Motherboards_S8030_S8030GM2NE_EN~Spec not 3.  If you don't have a way to test the other disconnected fan headers, that's fine, but lets make some assumptions that the other two fan headers are hooked to PWM 0 and 1 (given this starts at 3) and put that in the config.\n\nIf we're wrong, we can always fix it later once we have an acceptable fan setup, but I'd rather we not accentually break this later by adding fan headers."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/tyan/s8030_baseboard.json",
                    "line": 419,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Many Thanks for spending so much time on this review. Agree that the baseboard shall expose all the fan headers.\n\nI have pwm1 - pwm6 in hwmon. Adjusted the config to add the 5 headers. The extra headers don't cause any threshold event log since there is no reading so it's fine for me to have these enabled for now.\n\nAlso tested the pid loop again to confirm that the right pwm is written by pid control."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/tyan/s8030_baseboard.json",
                    "line": 419,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "FWIW, I physically have one of these boards behind me on the bench, so I have some incentive to get this right.\n\n>The extra headers don't cause any threshold event log since there is no \nreading so it's fine for me to have these enabled for now.\n\nIn general by default you should have no thresholds on a fan header, then apply the thresholds based on the detected chassis (and therefore fans).\n\n> Also tested the pid loop again to confirm that the right pwm is written by pid control.\n\nRock on."
                }
            ],
